      <section class="card" aria-labelledby="aboutMivoHeading" style="margin-top:18px">
        <h2 id="aboutMivoHeading">About Mivo</h2>
        <p>Mivo is a learning platform designed to help students organise their study goals, explore educational lessons, and practise topics connected with Nigerian school and entrance examinations. Students can choose a learning path, save subjects to a profile, and use the practice tools that are currently available on the platform.</p>
        <p>Mivo is an independent educational service. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by JAMB, WAEC, NECO, any university, or any government examination board. Students should confirm examination dates, syllabuses, registration rules and official results through the relevant official organisation.</p>
        <h3>How to use Mivo effectively</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Set a clear goal.</strong> Choose the examination or course you are preparing for and list the subjects or topics you need to cover.</li>
          <li><strong>Study in focused sessions.</strong> Pick one topic, study it without distractions, then take a short break before moving on.</li>
          <li><strong>Practise and review.</strong> Answer practice questions only after reviewing the topic. Read explanations where available and revisit questions you missed.</li>
          <li><strong>Track your progress honestly.</strong> Use scores to identify topics that need more attention; one practice result does not determine your ability.</li>
          <li><strong>Verify important information.</strong> Use official exam-board and school materials for final confirmation. Practice questions on Mivo are for learning and may contain errors.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3>Study guide: preparing for an examination</h3>
        <p><strong>Step 1 — Make a topic list:</strong> use the current official syllabus to break each subject into smaller topics. Mark each topic as not started, learning, or ready for revision.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 2 — Mix learning with retrieval practice:</strong> after reading a topic, close the notes and explain the main ideas in your own words. Then answer questions without looking at the answers first.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 3 — Keep an error log:</strong> write down the question topic, why your first answer was wrong, and the rule or idea that fixes the mistake. Review this log regularly.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 4 — Use timed practice carefully:</strong> practise with the time limits and question formats specified by your examination. Check your answers afterwards and spend more revision time on recurring weaknesses.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 5 — Protect rest and consistency:</strong> a realistic study timetable that includes sleep, breaks and revision is more useful than an extreme schedule that is difficult to maintain.</p>
        <h3>Contact and support</h3>
        <p>If you have a question about Mivo, privacy, or your account, email <a href="mailto:mohammeddanjumaah@gmail.com">mohammeddanjumaah@gmail.com</a>. Do not send passwords, one-time codes, or full payment credentials by email.</p>
        <p class="muted"><strong>Feature status:</strong> some functions may still be under development or depend on backend configuration. Mivo does not promise rewards, AI responses, payment processing, or other features unless they are visibly enabled and working.</p>
